==Post-match==
{{Quote box
| quoted = true
| bgcolor = #FFFFF0
| salign = center
| width = 27%
| align = right
| quote = Liverpool have their hands on the European Cup again, and this time [[European Champion Clubs' Cup#Clubs awarded the trophy permanently|it's for keeps!]] That trophy isn't going anywhere but Anfield. Liverpool become only the third club in the history of the competition to win it five times, and earn the right to keep the cup forever.
| source = [[Clive Tyldesley]] commentary on [[ITV (TV network)|ITV]] as Gerrard lifted the trophy.<ref>{{cite news |title=Champions League 25th Anniversary – Final – AC Milan 3–3 Liverpool (Liverpool won 3–2 on penalties) |url=http://www.itvsportarchive.com/index.php/news/11-archive-news/213-champions-league-25th-anniversary-final-ac-milan-3-3-liverpool |access-date=14 June 2021 |agency=ITV |archive-date=14 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210614133814/http://www.itvsportarchive.com/index.php/news/11-archive-news/213-champions-league-25th-anniversary-final-ac-milan-3-3-liverpool |url-status=dead}}</ref>
}}
Liverpool's triumph marked their fifth European Cup and the first by an English team since [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] had defeated Bayern Munich in the [[1999 UEFA Champions League final|1999 final]] in [[Barcelona]]. By winning the European Cup for a fifth time, Liverpool earned the privilege of wearing a [[European Champion Clubs' Cup#Multiple-winner badge|multiple-winner badge]] and the right to [[European Champion Clubs' Cup#Clubs awarded the trophy permanently|keep the trophy]] (under normal competition rules, the winning club can keep the trophy for only 10 months, as they must deliver it to UEFA two months before the next year's final). Liverpool were given ownership of the trophy every winner had held aloft since [[1995 UEFA Champions League final|1995]] (after Milan were permanently given the previous trophy after their fifth win in [[1994 UEFA Champions League final|1994]]).<ref>{{cite web |title=The UEFA Champions League trophy |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/finals/newsid=23694.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20090323040747/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/finals/newsid=23694.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=23 March 2009 |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=20 March 2009 |access-date=8 May 2009}}</ref> The [[2005–06 UEFA Champions League|2005–06]] participants competed for a new identical trophy. The rule to keep the trophy, which had been in effect since the [[1968–69 European Cup|1968–69 season]],<ref>{{cite web |title=The UEFA Champions League trophy |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/finals/newsid=23694.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20090323040747/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/finals/newsid=23694.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=23 March 2009 |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=20 March 2009 |access-date=8 May 2009}}</ref> was changed for the [[2008–09 UEFA Champions League|2008–09 season]] so that the actual trophy remained with UEFA at all times. Liverpool became the fifth and final club to be given this honour after Real Madrid, Ajax, Bayern Munich and Milan – all of whom had either won at least five times or three times consecutively.<ref name="UEFA honours">{{cite web|date=21 May 2018|title=How UEFA honours multiple European Cup winners|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0250-0c511b10d395-72f38b4cbce0-1000--how-uefa-honours-multiple-european-cup-winners/|website=[[UEFA]]|access-date=25 December 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170731191945/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2478341.html|archive-date=31 July 2017|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:CL parade Lpool.jpg|thumb|right|The Liverpool team parading the Champions League trophy in Liverpool city centre after their victory.]]

Liverpool celebrated their victory by parading the trophy around [[Liverpool]] in an open-top [[double-decker bus]] the day after the final. They were cheered by approximately 1 million supporters, with an estimated 300,000 fans located around [[St George's Hall, Liverpool|St George's Hall]] – the final destination of the parade.<ref name="Parade"/> Business experts estimated that one in five workers took time off following the victory. It was also estimated that Liverpudlians drank around 10,000 bottles of champagne after the match, with supermarket chain [[Sainsbury's]] stating: "We've never seen anything like it. We would usually expect to sell this much champagne at Christmas".<ref name="Parade">{{cite news |title=Victory tour seen by a million |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/england/merseyside/4582069.stm |work=BBC News |date=26 May 2005 |access-date=30 November 2008}}</ref>
[[File:2005 European Champion Clubs' Cup (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|left|The [[UEFA Champions League]] trophy won by Liverpool in Istanbul, on display in the club's museum a few weeks after the match.]]

Liverpool manager [[Rafael Benítez]] admitted after the match that the manner of his side's victory had stunned him and he stated: "My problem is that I don't have words to express the things that I feel at this moment".<ref>{{Cite news |title=Benitez stunned by epic comeback |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/4582585.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=26 May 2005 |access-date=1 December 2008}}</ref> Praise for Liverpool also came from outside England, including from Argentine legend [[Diego Maradona]], who said, "Even the Brazil team that won the [[1970 FIFA World Cup|1970 World Cup]] could not have staged a comeback with Milan leading 3–0... The English club proved that miracles really do exist. I've now made Liverpool my English team. They showed that football is the most beautiful sport of all. You knew they could defend, but the team showed they could play too and wrote a page in the history books. The match will last forever. The Liverpool supporters didn't let me go to sleep the night before. There were 10 of them to every three Milan supporters. They showed their unconditional support at half-time when they were losing 3–0 and still they didn't stop singing."<ref>{{cite web |title=History – The Miracle of Istanbul |url=http://www.liverpoolfc.com/history/the-miracle-of-istanbul |publisher=[[Liverpool F.C.]] |access-date=8 December 2020}}</ref> [[Johan Cruyff]] similarly praised the supporters after the game, saying "There's not one club in Europe with an anthem like "[[You'll Never Walk Alone]]." There's not one club in the world so united with the fans. I sat there watching the Liverpool fans and they sent shivers down my spine. A mass of 40,000 people became one force behind their team".<ref>{{cite news |first=Dan |last=Kay |title='I had never seen anything like this' - Johan Cruyff fell in love with Anfield but Liverpool dream was denied |url=https://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/i-never-seen-anything-like-24980285 |date=12 September 2022|access-date=5 July 2026 |work=Liverpool Echo}}</ref>

Benítez was also prepared to break up his winning side after the final with a number of players expected to leave the club to make way for new arrivals. One of those leaving was [[Vladimír Šmicer]], who had scored Liverpool's second goal in Istanbul, and whose contract was known not to be renewed before the final, meaning he entered knowing the final was his last game for the club. Milan were similarly astonished at the manner in which they had lost the final. Manager [[Carlo Ancelotti]] said, "We had six minutes of madness in which we threw away the position we had reached until then".<ref name=shattered>{{cite news |title=Ancelotti shattered after defeat |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/4574893.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=25 May 2005 |access-date=1 December 2008}}</ref> The result compounded Milan's failure to win [[Serie A]] a week before the match. Milan's vice-president, [[Adriano Galliani]], played down the loss, asserting: "Even if we come second in the league, and second in the Champions League, this is not a disastrous season for us". Captain [[Paolo Maldini]] was less optimistic, stating that the reverse was a "huge disappointment", but he added that Milan would accept the defeat and "go out with their heads high".<ref name=shattered/>

[[File:Liverpool 2005 ucl medal.jpg|thumb|upright|Liverpool shirt from the final and a winners medal in the club's museum]]

Much discussion after the final centered on the future of Liverpool captain [[Steven Gerrard]] who had been linked with a move to rivals [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]]. Gerrard stated in the immediate aftermath of the victory, "How can I think of leaving Liverpool after a night like this?"<ref>{{cite news |title=Gerrard could stay after Euro win |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/4578603.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=26 May 2005 |access-date=30 November 2008}}</ref> Media reports then quoted Gerrard as saying he wished to leave Liverpool, citing events that had occurred in the month after the Champions League victory as the reason. On 6 July, however, Liverpool chief executive [[Rick Parry]] released a statement confirming that Gerrard would remain at the club,<ref>{{cite news |title=Gerrard in shock Liverpool U-turn |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/l/liverpool/4654837.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=6 July 2005 |access-date=1 December 2008}}</ref> and Gerrard signed a new four-year contract on 8 July.<ref>{{cite news |title=The Steven Gerrard saga |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/l/liverpool/4651687.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=10 July 2005 |access-date=30 November 2008}}</ref> He ultimately never played for another club in Europe, as he departed for the [[Los Angeles Galaxy]] of [[Major League Soccer]] a decade later.<ref>{{cite news |title=Galaxy announces Steven Gerrard's 18-month deal on Wednesday |url=http://www.latimes.com/sports/soccer/la-sp-galaxy-steven-gerrard-20150107-story.html |date=7 January 2015 |first=Kevin |last=Baxter|access-date=21 January 2018 |newspaper=Los Angeles Times}}</ref>

Despite winning the competition and the precedent that the holders defend their trophy, Liverpool's place in next season's Champions League was still in doubt. Prior to the 2005 Champions League final, [[The Football Association]] (FA) of England had decided on 5 May that only the top four finishers in the Premier League would qualify and Liverpool ended their domestic season in fifth place behind [[Everton F.C.|Everton]].<ref>{{cite news |title=FA decision goes against Liverpool |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2005/SPORT/football/05/05/england.liverpool/index.html |date=5 May 2005 |access-date=24 July 2026 |publisher=CNN}}</ref> The governing body of the competition [[UEFA]] initially maintained that each country could only have four Champions League spots and suggested that the FA could nominate Liverpool instead of Everton.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://archive.anfieldroad.com/news-archive/200505271310/uefa-and-fa-argue-about-liverpools-defence.html/ |title=UEFA and FA argue about Liverpool's defence |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160116035148/http://archive.anfieldroad.com/news-archive/200505271310/uefa-and-fa-argue-about-liverpools-defence.html/ |archive-date=16 January 2016 |url-status=dead |website=Anfield Road.com |date=27 May 2005 |access-date=2 November 2019}}</ref> Liverpool faced a three-week wait to discover if they would be allowed to defend their title as all previous winners of the competition had done. UEFA came to a decision on 10 June, confirming that both Everton and Liverpool would be able to compete in the Champions League qualifying rounds; however, Liverpool were entered into the first qualifying round, and were given no "country protection"; meaning they could face another English club at any stage of the competition.<ref>{{cite web |title=Liverpool allowed to defend title |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefa/news/Kind=128/newsId=308644.html |publisher=UEFA |date=10 June 2005 |access-date=30 November 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070621190250/http://www.uefa.com/uefa/news/Kind%3D128/newsId%3D308644.html |archive-date=21 June 2007 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Liverpool were seeded meaning at the [[2005–06 UEFA Champions League qualifying rounds#Third qualifying round|third qualifying round]] they could have faced the unseeded Everton (who ultimately faced Villarreal and were knocked out), while UEFA chief executive [[Lars-Christer Olsson]] said there was never any discussion of whether Liverpool should enter the competition as it was unanimous as holders they should.<ref name="Olsson">{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/l/liverpool/4613695.stm |title=Liverpool get in Champions League |website=BBC Sport |date=10 June 2005 |access-date=11 December 2007 |archive-date=12 April 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120412074435/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/l/liverpool/4613695.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> To ensure no repeat the UEFA Executive Committee also amended the regulations for future competitions so that the holders would have the right to defend their title automatically, though at the expense of the lowest placed team in those countries that had more than one qualifier.<ref name="Olsson"/><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2005/jun/10/newsstory.sport8 |title=Reds given right to defend crown |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=10 June 2005 |access-date=10 February 2025}}</ref>

As champions, Liverpool faced [[PFC CSKA Moscow|CSKA Moscow]] (winners of the [[2005 UEFA Cup Final]]) in the [[2005 UEFA Super Cup]], held on 26 August. Liverpool won the match 3–1 after extra time.<ref>{{cite news |title=Liverpool 3–1 CSKA Moscow (aet) |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/4180288.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=26 August 2005 |access-date=30 November 2008}}</ref> Liverpool's victory in Istanbul also meant they qualified for the [[2005 FIFA Club World Championship]]. Liverpool beat [[Deportivo Saprissa S.A.D.|Deportivo Saprissa]] 3–0 in the semi-final, and played [[Copa Libertadores]] champions [[São Paulo FC|São Paulo]] in the [[2005 FIFA Club World Championship final|final]], losing 1–0.<ref>{{cite news |title=São Paulo 1–0 Liverpool |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/4533980.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=18 December 2005 |access-date=30 November 2008}}</ref>

Both teams retained a number of players who played in this final for the rematch in the [[2007 UEFA Champions League final|2007 final]] in Athens, which Milan won 2–1. Many players who played in this game also played for their clubs' legends teams, Liverpool Legends and Milan Glorie, in Liverpool's 2019 Legends Game at Anfield; [[Robbie Fowler]] and Cissé put Liverpool 2–0 up before Pirlo and [[Giuseppe Pancaro]] brought Milan level. Gerrard then scored in the final minute of play to give Liverpool a 3–2 win. The match raised an estimated £1&nbsp;million for the LFC Foundation.<ref>{{cite news |first=Ian |last=Doyle |title=Liverpool Legends beat AC Milan in thrilling 3–2 win as one former star steals the show |url=https://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/liverpool-legends-beat-ac-milan-16018455 |work=Liverpool Echo}}</ref>

{{blockquote|It was the six minutes that shook the world. It was the six minutes that so stunned AC Milan that Carlo Ancelotti's devastated players threw away their losers' medals at the end. Orchestrated by the determination of Steven Gerrard, the tactical cunning of Rafa Benítez and the support of the fans who demanded that each player perform with pride in the shirt, these six minutes showed you should never give up. Even now, a familiar exhortation will go around Liverpool players or supporters when they walk through a storm. "Remember Istanbul".|[[Henry Winter]] in ''[[The Daily Telegraph|The Telegraph]]'' (December 2009), Top 20 sporting moments of the decade: Liverpool's 'Miracle of Istanbul'.<ref name="Winter"/>}}

In a 2011 poll conducted by ''UEFA.com'', Dudek's double save from Shevchenko in the 117th minute was voted the greatest Champions League moment of all time, ahead of [[Zinedine Zidane]]'s left-footed volley against [[Bayer 04 Leverkusen|Bayer Leverkusen]] in the [[2002 UEFA Champions League final|2002 final]] and [[Ole Gunnar Solskjær]]'s injury-time winner against [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] in [[1999 UEFA Champions League final|1999]] for Manchester United.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/polls/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130924234142/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/polls/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=24 September 2013 |title=The greatest Champions League moments |publisher=UEFA |date=15 September 2011|access-date=4 July 2019}}</ref> On the second save from inside 3 yards, Dudek wrote in his autobiography: "They called the 2005 Champions League final the 'Miracle of Istanbul'. Well, this was my miracle. The save of my career. Of my life."<ref name="Double save">{{cite news |title=The story behind Jerzy Dudek's double save which led to Liverpool's Champions League triumph |url=https://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/story-behind-jerzy-dudeks-double-18307738 |access-date=18 September 2020 |newspaper=Liverpool Echo}}</ref> When asked to explain his saves after the game, an emotional Dudek said he took inspiration from [[Pope John Paul II]] (a fellow Pole and goalkeeper in his youth) who died in April, the month before the final. "I'm dedicating this to the memory of John Paul. I had contact with him during his life and I've felt the inspiration since his death. I can't account for it."<ref>{{cite news |title=Dudek dedicates medal to late Pope |url=https://www.standard.co.uk/sport/dudek-dedicates-medal-to-late-pope-7186060.html |date=26 May 2005 |access-date=10 December 2020 |newspaper=Evening Standard}}</ref> Dudek dubbed it the "Hand of Pope", an allusion to Diego Maradona dubbing his infamous first goal in the [[Argentina v England (1986 FIFA World Cup)|1986 World Cup quarter final]] the "[[The hand of God|Hand of God]]".<ref>{{cite news |title=Jerzy Dudek interview: How 'Hand of Pope' sealed Liverpool's Champions League miracle in Istanbul |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/05/31/jerzy-dudek-interview-hand-pope-sealed-liverpools-champions/ |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220112/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/05/31/jerzy-dudek-interview-hand-pope-sealed-liverpools-champions/ |archive-date=12 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |access-date=14 December 2020 |newspaper=The Telegraph}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

== In popular culture ==
The 2014 British comedy movie ''[[One Night in Istanbul]]'' is set in Istanbul on the night of the final, and includes some footage from the match.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.liverpoolfc.com/news/latest-news/168516-official-trailer-one-night-in-istanbul |title=Official trailer: 'One Night in Istanbul' |publisher=Liverpool FC |date=8 August 2014|access-date=10 August 2014}}</ref>

The 2011 British sports drama ''[[Will (2011 film)|Will]]'' (starring Liverpool fan [[Damian Lewis]]) centres on the trials and tribulations in the lives of two main fictional characters: eleven-year-old Will Brennan and Bosnian footballer Alek, and their trek to see Liverpool play Milan in the 2005 Champions League final.<ref>{{cite news |title=Liverpool fan Damian Lewis on starring with his Anfield heroes in new film |url=https://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/entertainment/celebrity/liverpool-fan-damian-lewis-on-starring-1085574 |access-date=15 May 2022 |work=Daily Record}}</ref>

The final is discussed in author John Green's podcast ''[[The Anthropocene Reviewed]],'' and the essay was later adapted for Green's book of the same name''.''<ref>{{Cite web |title=You'll Never Walk Alone and Jerzy Dudek {{!}} The Anthropocene Reviewed |url=https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/anthropocene-reviewed/episodes/anthropocene-reviewed-youll-never-walk-alone-and-jerzy-dudek |access-date=2022-03-28 |website=WNYC Studios |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |first=John |last=Green |title=The Anthropocene Reviewed |date=2021 |publisher=Penguin Publishing Group |isbn=978-0-525-55522-3 |oclc=1260105845}}</ref>

The final is an important shared memory between the main characters of the 2017 Korean romantic drama series ''[[Rain or Shine (TV series)|Rain or Shine]]''.<ref>{{Cite episode |title=그냥 사랑하는 |url=https://mtv.jtbc.co.kr/rainshine|access-date= 5 August 2024 |series=그냥 사랑하는 |network=JTBC |date=2 January 2018 |season=1 |number=7}}</ref>

